Oops - my bad. I thought I deleted that, but I may as well continue it 
here anyway.
If one believes that a switch is not on, it doesn't necessarily mean 
that the switch is off: the switch could be broken or stuck halfway. But 
in the case of the existence of an object, it is a binary decision: it 
either exists or it doesn't exist. There is no alternative.

An example. If I say to you "I am not in the library", it is 
unreasonable to say "You are therefore in your house", the reason being, 
there are more places than just my house and the library. If I say "I 
don't believe in god", then it IS reasonable to say that I believe that 
god doesn't exist because there is no alternative.

Now, you may argue that there is indeed an alternative: not believe that 
god exists, but not believe that god doesn't exist. This is not an 
answer. It is equivalent to not having an opinion. It is saying "I am 
undecided."

When asked, "Does god exist?" one can either say "Yes", "No", or refuse 
to answer.

